{"from": "artifact:tel-dan-stele", "type": "attests", "to": "person:david", "grade": "A", "note": "An Old Aramaic royal inscription boasting of victory over the king of Israel and the king of the \"House of David\" (bytdwd); the earliest widely accepted extra-biblical reference to David as founder of a dynasty."}
{"from": "artifact:tel-dan-stele", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-06-david-king", "grade": "B", "note": "Names a \"king of the House of David\" (reconstructed as Ahaziah) about a century after David; attests the dynasty, not David himself."}
{"from": "artifact:tel-dan-stele", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-01-jesus-is-the-christ", "grade": "B", "note": "Attests that a royal \"House of David\" existed, the line into which Matthew places Jesus as \"son of David\"."}
{"from": "artifact:mesha-stele", "type": "attests", "to": "person:david", "grade": "D", "note": "Lemaire reads line 31 (BT[D]WD) as \"House of David\", but Richelle and Burlingame hold the reading uncertain, so the Mesha reference to David is disputed."}
{"from": "artifact:sennacherib-annals", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-09-hezekiah-king", "grade": "A", "note": "Names \"the king of Judah, Hezekiah, who had not submitted to my authority\" and the capture of forty-six of his fortified cities in 701 BCE."}
{"from": "artifact:sennacherib-annals", "type": "attests", "to": "person:hezekiah", "grade": "A", "note": "Names Hezekiah, king of Judah, and says \"I shut him up like a caged bird in his royal city of Jerusalem\"."}
{"from": "artifact:hezekiah-bulla", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-09-hezekiah-king", "grade": "A", "note": "Seal impression \"Belonging to Hezekiah [son of] Ahaz king of Judah\", found in 2015 in a scientific excavation."}
{"from": "artifact:hezekiah-bulla", "type": "attests", "to": "person:hezekiah", "grade": "A", "note": "Bulla reading \"Belonging to Hezekiah [son of] Ahaz king of Judah\"."}
{"from": "artifact:lachish-reliefs", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-09-hezekiah-king", "grade": "B", "note": "Palace reliefs narrating Sennacherib's victory over the kingdom of Judah at the siege of Lachish in 701 BCE, the campaign context of Hezekiah's reign; they do not name Hezekiah."}
{"from": "artifact:lachish-reliefs", "type": "attests", "to": "person:hezekiah", "grade": "B", "note": "Depict the Assyrian campaign against Judah of 701 BCE, during Hezekiah's reign; they do not name him."}
{"from": "artifact:ahaz-bulla", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-09-ahaz-king", "grade": "D", "note": "Seal impression \"Belonging to Ahaz (son of) Yehotam, King of Judah\"; unprovenanced (mid-1990s antiquities market) but, as the source notes, bullae are considered virtually impossible to forge. Graded D because the bulla is unprovenanced (architect review, 2026-11-10)."}
{"from": "artifact:ahaz-bulla", "type": "attests", "to": "person:ahaz", "grade": "D", "note": "Bulla reading \"Belonging to Ahaz (son of) Yehotam, King of Judah\". Graded D because the bulla is unprovenanced (architect review, 2026-11-10)."}
{"from": "artifact:tiglath-pileser-inscriptions", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-09-ahaz-king", "grade": "A", "note": "The Tiglath-Pileser III annals record tributes and payments received from \"Ahaz, king of Judah\"."}
{"from": "artifact:tiglath-pileser-inscriptions", "type": "attests", "to": "person:ahaz", "grade": "A", "note": "Annals of Tiglath-Pileser III name tribute from Ahaz, king of Judah."}
{"from": "artifact:uzziah-ministers-bullae", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-08-uzziah-king", "grade": "D", "note": "Contemporary seal impressions reading \"[belonging] to X, minister of Uziyah\" - \"the first authentic contemporary attestations to the ancient king\"; graded D because the source states they are of unprovenanced origin (antiquities market, not a recorded excavation), so authenticity cannot be secured (reviewer 1, 2026-11-11)."}
{"from": "artifact:uzziah-ministers-bullae", "type": "attests", "to": "person:uzziah", "grade": "D", "note": "Seal impressions of officials \"of Uziyah\", called the first authentic contemporary attestations to the king; graded D because they are unprovenanced (reviewer 1, 2026-11-11)."}
{"from": "artifact:uzziah-epitaph", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-08-uzziah-king", "grade": "D", "note": "Reburial epitaph \"Hither were brought the bones of Uzziah, king of Judah\"; its script dates it to around 30-70 CE, roughly 700 years after his death, and its provenance is undocumented - so it is not a contemporary record."}
{"from": "artifact:uzziah-epitaph", "type": "attests", "to": "person:uzziah", "grade": "D", "note": "Names Uzziah, king of Judah, in a Second Temple-period reburial inscription dated c. 30-70 CE, not a contemporary record."}
{"from": "artifact:esarhaddon-inscriptions", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-10-manasseh-king", "grade": "A", "note": "Assyrian records list Manasseh among twenty-two vassal kings required to provide materials for Esarhaddon's building projects; Ashurbanipal also names him."}
{"from": "artifact:esarhaddon-inscriptions", "type": "attests", "to": "person:manasseh", "grade": "A", "note": "Manasseh named in the vassal lists of Esarhaddon and Ashurbanipal."}
{"from": "artifact:jehoiachin-ration-tablets", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-11-jeconiah-king", "grade": "A", "note": "Tablets list rations \"to the king of Judah, Yaukin\" (Jehoiachin) and his five sons in Babylon, about five years after the 597 BCE captivity."}
{"from": "artifact:jehoiachin-ration-tablets", "type": "attests", "to": "person:jeconiah", "grade": "A", "note": "Names Jehoiachin (Ya'ukinu) and his five sons as recipients of rations in Babylon."}
{"from": "artifact:jehoiachin-ration-tablets", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-11-exile-to-babylon", "grade": "A", "note": "The deposed king of Judah is listed on ration tablets in Babylon, five years after his captivity - direct evidence of the exile of 597 BCE."}
{"from": "artifact:nebuchadnezzar-chronicle", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-11-exile-to-babylon", "grade": "A", "note": "The Babylonian Chronicle records Nebuchadnezzar's siege and capture of Jerusalem in 597 BCE, in which Jeconiah surrendered and was taken to Babylon."}
{"from": "artifact:nebuchadnezzar-chronicle", "type": "attests", "to": "person:jeconiah", "grade": "B", "note": "Records the 597 BCE capture of Jerusalem, after which Nebuchadnezzar installed Zedekiah and kept Jeconiah in Babylon."}
{"from": "artifact:cyrus-cylinder", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-12-return-after-exile", "grade": "C", "note": "Cyrus is extolled as one who \"repatriated displaced people\" - the imperial context of the return from exile; the Cylinder does not name Judah, Jews or Jerusalem."}
{"from": "artifact:bubastite-portal", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-07-rehoboam-king", "grade": "B", "note": "Shoshenq I, generally identified with the biblical Shishak, left a conquest list over Israel/Judah-region towns from the era of Rehoboam; it does not name Rehoboam."}
{"from": "artifact:great-isaiah-scroll", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-22-isaiah-7-14-quoted", "grade": "B", "note": "The Hebrew text of Isaiah, including chapter 7, is preserved complete from ca. 125 BCE - predating Matthew and showing the wording of the verse he quotes."}
{"from": "text:josephus-antiquities-20", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-01-jesus-is-the-christ", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus (c. 94 CE) refers to \"Jesus, who was called the Christ\" in his account of James - the source names the person with the title the claim records; independent, non-Christian evidence that Jesus was known as \"Christ\" (grade A per reviewer-1 calibration, 2026-11-11)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-antiquities-20", "type": "attests", "to": "person:jesus-of-nazareth", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus names Jesus as a historical figure, the brother of James, called the Christ - a source that names the person is grade A (reviewer-1 calibration, 2026-11-11); the minor authenticity debate over the passage does not reach grade D."}
{"from": "text:erusin-betrothal-law", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-01-19-betrothal-put-away", "grade": "C", "note": "Jewish law: after erusin (betrothal) the laws of adultery apply and the union \"can only be ended by the same divorce process as married couples\" - the \"putting away\" Joseph considered. Sources are rabbinic, codified after the first century."}
